 ## check.sh
 
 ### §check-fails-open — the check never blocks a commit because of its own failure
+- Status: SUPERSEDED 2026-10-23 by §check-fails-closed (below).
 - Since: 2026-09-11
-- Decision: `set -uo pipefail` without `-e`. Every internal error still ends in `exit 0`; the script only warns.
-- Forbidden: `set -e` or `set -o errexit`, and any exit status other than 0.
+- Decision (superseded): `set -uo pipefail` without `-e`. Every internal error still ends in `exit 0`; the script only warns.
+- Forbidden (superseded): `set -e` or `set -o errexit`, and any exit status other than 0.
 - Reason: with `set -e`, a `wc` failure on a staged-then-deleted file aborted the check and blocked every commit in
   the repository for a day (2026-09-10). The check is advisory by design.
+
+### §check-fails-closed — any failing command aborts the check with a non-zero exit
+- Since: 2026-10-23
+- Supersedes: §check-fails-open. The maintainer explicitly overrode that decision on 2026-10-23.
+- Decision: `set -euo pipefail`. Any failing command aborts the check with a non-zero exit. Large staged files are
+  still only warned about (exit 0); the non-zero exit is for the check's own failures.
+- Note: tests that are expected to be false must not be the last command of a `&&` list (use `if`), or `set -e`
+  turns a normal "file is under the limit" into an abort.
+- Risk (known, from the superseded record): a `wc` failure on a staged-then-deleted file blocked commits on
+  2026-09-10. The `[ -f "$f" ] || continue` guard covers that case; other failures now block by design.
